args.description:
In the SQLite database, investigate the `is_deleted` column in the `jobs` table. Run these queries and report results:

1. SELECT is_deleted, COUNT(*) FROM jobs GROUP BY is_deleted;
2. For jobs in region 'eu-north' (join jobs j -> nodes n ON j.node_id=n.node_id -> clusters c ON n.cluster_id=c.cluster_id WHERE c.region='eu-north'), report: SELECT j.is_deleted, COUNT(*) FROM ... GROUP BY j.is_deleted;
3. For eu-north jobs with is_deleted=1, what are their statuses? SELECT LOWER(j.status), COUNT(*) FROM ... WHERE c.region='eu-north' AND j.is_deleted=1 GROUP BY LOWER(j.status);
4. Check if there's a jobs_v1 table and whether it has is_deleted. Compare: does jobs_v1 have the same job_ids? Is jobs_v1 the "current" table or an older one? SELECT COUNT(*) FROM jobs; SELECT COUNT(*) FROM jobs_v1; Also check if jobs_v1 has an is_deleted column.
5. Look at whether is_deleted=1 jobs have ended_at values (suggesting they were completed/terminated) vs is_deleted=0.

Report all findings clearly. Do NOT compute the final percentage answer.
